        <p data-pasted="true">Though the City has created a balanced budget each year, unfunded state mandates and increased costs have impacted the budget, and now the scope of the City&rsquo;s needs is greater than the current resources available. Despite the City&rsquo;s fiscal conservatism, Hemet continues to face increased service demands, infrastructure needs, and personnel costs.</p><p>The City is currently using reserves to balance the budget and has a structural deficit projected to reach several million dollars in the coming years. Without a new revenue stream, the cuts will become more severe in the next fiscal year.</p><p>To review the City presentation about the budget, visit, <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12614" target="_blank" rel="noopener">July 14, 2026 City PowerPoint Presentation</a>.</p><h2 class="subhead1">Measure Q: A Sales Tax on the November 3, 2026 Ballot</h2><p data-pasted="true">To maintain and secure financial stability, preserve the City&rsquo;s public safety, city services, and local infrastructure, the Hemet City Council recently voted to place Measure Q, a one-cent sales tax measure, on the November 2026, ballot. Measure Q could provide local funding for general use, such as:</p><ul><li>Improve 9-1-1 emergency and fire response</li><li>Maintain police services, including neighborhood police patrols, crime prevention, and helping address homelessness</li><li>Maintain fire protection services, improve infrastructure, and replace outdated lifesaving and firefighting equipment</li><li>Maintain library services</li><li>Attract and retain businesses</li><li>Support safe, clean drinking water and water infrastructure</li><li>Repair streets, fix potholes, and keep public areas clean and safe by removing trash and graffiti</li></ul><h2 class="subhead1">Fiscal Accountability and Local Control</h2><p>Measure Q would require fiscal accountability protections. Currently, a majority of the sales tax generated locally goes to the State or the County &ndash; all money raised by the potential measure would stay in Hemet to address local needs. By law, no funds could be taken away by the State. A clear system of accountability would be required, including public disclosure of spending, independent citizens&rsquo; oversight, and audits to ensure that the money is spent as voters intend.&nbsp;</p><h3 class="subhead2">Essential purchases such as groceries, prescription medicine, diapers, and feminine hygiene products are exempt from sales tax.</h3><h2 class="subhead1"><br></h2><h2 class="subhead1">LINKS:</h2><ul><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12615" target="_blank" rel="noopener">English Fact Sheet</a> | <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12616" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spanish Fact Sheet</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12627" target="_blank" rel="noopener">English FAQ</a> | <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12650" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spanish FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12619" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resolution</a> | <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12651" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Exhibit A - Supp Tax Measure Ordinance</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12678" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City Attorney Impartial Analysis</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12680" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argument in Favor of Measure Q</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;<a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13032" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rebuttal to Argument in Favor of Measure Q</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/12679" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argument Against Measure Q</a>&nbsp; &nbsp; <a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13031" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rebuttal to Argument Against Measure Q</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13208" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hemet Finance Department Presentation</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13209" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hemet Fire Department Presentation</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13210" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hemet Police Department Presentation</a></li><li><a href="/DocumentCenter/View/13211" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hemet Public Works Department Presentation</a></li></ul>
